## What the data say before interpretation

For May 2025, BLS reports:

- automotive service technician/mechanic national median: **$50,620**;
- national top-10-percent threshold: **$81,790**;
- motor-vehicle-and-parts-dealer technician median: **$58,140**.

The U.S. Department of Labor separately explains that a dealership “flat rate” hour is **not an actual clock hour**. A technician's paid/credited hours can differ from the number of hours physically spent at work.

Those facts do not prove that $120,000 is impossible. They show why **“there are $120,000 jobs” is not yet an adequate description of the opportunity offered to a new entrant.**

## Blackball rule

Whenever somebody says “these jobs are sitting there and people won't take them,” collect at least:

1. vacancy count and denominator;
2. exact pay-plan definition;
3. starting and median pay, not just maximum/high performer;
4. years and money required to qualify;
5. worker-supplied capital/tools;
6. schedule and physical cost;
7. who bears downtime/demand/rework risk;
8. five-year retention;
9. geographic availability;
10. whether the claimed earnings are guaranteed, typical, conditional, or merely possible.

Apply the same rule to universities, apprenticeships, trade schools, boot camps, employers, military recruiting, franchising, and entrepreneurship pitches.

A shortage can be real while the advertised opportunity is badly described. A high-paying opportunity can also be real while being rare, difficult to enter, or difficult to sustain. The job is to measure which.
